<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eadberht of Northumbria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/768">768</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eadberht (died 768) was king of Northumbria from 737 to 758, ruling in partnership with his brother Ecgbert, archbishop of York. He issued coins in his own name, annexed Kyle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a> in 750 and in 756, with the Pictish king Óengus, forced the Britons of Dumbarton to submit, though his army was destroyed soon after. He abdicated in 758 to become a monk at York.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eadberht-of-northumbria.jpg" alt="Eadberht of Northumbria" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eadberht_sceat_737_75001443.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eadberht was the son of Eata, whom the genealogy gives the cognomen Glin Mawr, and a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ida-of-bernicia">Ida of Bernicia</a>, through Ida's son Ocga according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> and the Anglian collection, or through Eadric according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a>. He came to the throne after the second abdication of his cousin Ceolwulf, who entered the monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>. Ceolwulf's first abdication had clearly involved force, but the second, in Eadberht's favour, may have been voluntary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rival families opposed Eadberht throughout his reign. Eardwine, probably the son of King Eadwulf, was killed in 740. In 750 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>, son of King Aldfrith, was taken from the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> and put to death after a siege, and Bishop Cynewulf of Lindisfarne, who had presumably supported him, was deposed and held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>. The great churches took sides in these feuds: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a> was linked to Eardwine's family, Lindisfarne to Offa and Ceolwulf, while Eadberht had the support of his brother, the Archbishop of York.</p><p>The reign saw major reforms of the Northumbrian coinage, and the historian Kirby judged that Eadberht was bringing new prosperity to his kingdom. A letter of Pope Paul I ordered the king and the archbishop to return lands taken from Abbot Fothred, which suggests attempts to reclaim some of the vast estates granted to the church in earlier reigns.</p><p>Kirby also saw at Eadberht's court a revival of seventh-century northern imperial ambitions. In 740 a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> took him north, and Æthelbald, King of Mercia, ravaged his lands in his absence. The campaign of 756 ended at a place a chronicle calls Niwanbirig, usually placed at Newburgh-on-Tyne near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexham">Hexham</a>; another reading puts it at Newborough by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a>, which would make the disaster a defeat by the Mercians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eadberht entered the monastery attached to York cathedral, and Symeon of Durham records his death there in 768 and his burial in the cathedral porch beside his brother Ecgbert, who had died in 766. His son Oswulf succeeded him but was murdered within the year. His daughter Osgifu's husband Alhred then became king, and Eadberht's descendants contested the throne until the end of the century. The last known of them, Osgifu's son Saint Alhmund, was murdered in 800 on the orders of King Eardwulf and was reputed a martyr.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eadberht of Northumbria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Eadberht</b> (737–758) took Kyle in Ayrshire and in 756, with the Pictish king Óengus, forced the Britons of Dumbarton to submit, but lost most of his army shortly afterwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-minster">York Minster</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.96,-1.08&amp;year=758" aria-label="York Minster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Eadberht of Northumbria"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4946 5515h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4946 5515h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.96,-1.08&amp;year=758">What was here in 758 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
